    "evidence_text": "Instability and decay of the primary structure of DNA\n\nAlthough DNA is the carrier of genetic information, it has limited chemical stability.\n\nDNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types\n\nHigh-throughput sequencing reveals extensive variation in human-specific L1 content in individual human genomes.\n\nRibosomal DNA copy number loss and sequence variation in cancer\n\nRibosomal DNA is one of the most variable regions in the human genome with respect to copy number.\n\nWe find that copy number and sequence can change in cancer genomes.\n\nLoss of copies occurs early and is associated with hypersensitivity to DNA damage.\n\nCytochemistry and C-values: the less-well-known world of nuclear DNA amounts.\n\nPlant slimes are another source of concern.   \n\n\nAutoradiography of chromosomal DNA fibers from Chinese hamster cells.\n\nThe EMBL nucleotide sequence database.\n\nDNA and RNA sequences are directly submitted from researchers and genome sequencing groups and collected from the scientific literature and patent applications (Fig. 1).\n\nDatabase releases are produced quarterly and are distributed on CD-ROM.\n\nMetabolic DNA as the origin of spontaneously released DNA?\n\nMitochondrial genome variation and the origin of modern humans.\n\nGenome downsizing in polyploid plants\n\nThe nature of the evolutionary forces that may be driving DNA loss are also discussed.",
